BT /F1 8.0 Tf 0.10 0.10 0.10 rg 46.0 733.0 Td (Distributed systems frequently encounter transient network dropouts: a client transmits an API request, the server executes it and) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 0.10 0.10 0.10 rg 46.0 722.0 Td (writes to the database, but the network connection terminates before the HTTP response reaches the caller. If the client retries) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 0.10 0.10 0.10 rg 46.0 711.0 Td (without idempotency controls, the customer is double-billed. The engine implements a strict RFC-compliant `Idempotency-Key`) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 0.10 0.10 0.10 rg 46.0 700.0 Td (protocol.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 0.10 0.10 0.10 rg 46.0 686.0 Td (Upon receiving a request, the server computes a SHA-256 digest of the raw request payload. It inspects `idempotency_records`) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 0.10 0.10 0.10 rg 46.0 675.0 Td (within an atomic transaction. If the idempotency key exists and the payload hash matches, the server returns the cached status) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 0.10 0.10 0.10 rg 46.0 664.0 Td (code and response body immediately, setting `replay: true` without incrementing tenant counters or charging the customer. If the) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 0.10 0.10 0.10 rg 46.0 653.0 Td (key exists with a conflicting payload, HTTP 409 Conflict is returned.) Tj ET
